import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Scanner;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        ArrayStack<String> stack = new ArrayStack<>();
        while (sc.hasNext()) {
            stack.push(sc.next());
        }

        if (stack.isEmpty()) {
            System.out.println("Empty stack");
            return;
        }
        Pair<String, Integer> top = new Pair<>(stack.peek(), stack.size());
        System.out.println("Top: " + top);
        StringBuilder reversed = new StringBuilder();
        while (!stack.isEmpty()) {
            if (reversed.length() > 0) {
                reversed.append(' ');
            }
            reversed.append(stack.pop());
        }
        System.out.println(reversed);
    }
}

class ArrayStack<T> {
    private final List<T> items = new ArrayList<>();

    void push(T item) {
        items.add(item);
    }

    T pop() {
        return items.remove(items.size() - 1);
    }

    T peek() {
        return items.get(items.size() - 1);
    }

    boolean isEmpty() {
        return items.isEmpty();
    }

    int size() {
        return items.size();
    }
}

class Pair<A, B> {
    private final A first;
    private final B second;

    Pair(A first, B second) {
        this.first = first;
        this.second = second;
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return "(" + first + ", " + second + ")";
    }
}

steps.body:
A **generic method** declares its own type parameter before the return type. The parameter and return types can then use it.

```java
static <T> T lastOf(List<T> items) {
    return items.get(items.size() - 1);
}
```

You never pass `T` yourself. At the call site the compiler **infers** it from the arguments: `lastOf(words)` with a `List<String>` makes `T` a `String`, so the result is a `String` with no cast. Call it again with a `List<Integer>` and `T` is `Integer`.

Declare several parameters the same way:

```java harness
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    {{code}}
    {
        return map.containsKey(key) ? map.get(key) : fallback;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        Map<String, Integer> lengths = new TreeMap<>(Map.of("fig", 3, "apple", 5));
        System.out.println("fig -> " + getOr(lengths, "fig", 0));
        System.out.println("plum -> " + getOr(lengths, "plum", 0));
    }
}
```

```java
static <K, V> V getOr(Map<K, V> map, K key, V fallback)
```

Inside the method you only know that `T` is some object. You can call `equals` and `toString`, and `+` can join it into a String, but you cannot call `length()` or do math with it. Compare values with `equals`, never `==`. On objects, `==` asks whether two references point to the same object, and two Integers holding 300 are separate objects because the cache only covers -128 to 127.

