import java.io.BufferedReader;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.io.InputStreamReader;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.TreeMap;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
        BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(System.in));
        List<String> words = new ArrayList<>();
        for (String part : br.readLine().strip().split("\\s+")) {
            words.add(part);
        }
        List<Integer> nums = new ArrayList<>();
        for (String part : br.readLine().strip().split("\\s+")) {
            nums.add(Integer.parseInt(part));
        }
        Map<String, Integer> counts = new TreeMap<>();
        for (String w : words) {
            counts.merge(w, 1, Integer::sum);
        }

        String first = words.get(0);
        System.out.println(first + ": " + countMatches(words, first));
        System.out.println(nums.get(0) + ": " + countMatches(nums, nums.get(0)));
        System.out.println("Counts: " + counts);
        System.out.println("Count 1: " + keyFor(counts, 1));
        System.out.println("Count 2: " + keyFor(counts, 2));
    }

    static <T> int countMatches(List<T> items, T target) {
        int count = 0;
        for (T item : items) {
            if (item.equals(target)) {
                count++;
            }
        }
        return count;
    }

    static <K, V> K keyFor(Map<K, V> map, V value) {
        for (Map.Entry<K, V> entry : map.entrySet()) {
            if (entry.getValue().equals(value)) {
                return entry.getKey();
            }
        }
        return null;
    }
}

steps.body:
A plain `T` could be anything, so you cannot call `compareTo` on it. A **bound** narrows it: `<T extends Comparable<T>>` means `T` must be comparable to itself. In a bound, `extends` covers interfaces too. String, Integer, Double, and your own `Comparable` classes such as `Version` all qualify, and now `item.compareTo(best)` compiles.

```java harness
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    {{code}}
    {
        T best = items.get(0);
        for (T item : items) {
            if (item.compareTo(best) > 0) {
                best = item;
            }
        }
        return best;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        System.out.println("Max word: " + maxOf(List.of("pear", "fig", "plum")));
        System.out.println("Max number: " + maxOf(List.of(4, 19, 7)));
    }
}
```

```java
static <T extends Comparable<T>> T maxOf(List<T> items)
```

Generics are **invariant**: a `List<Integer>` is not a `List<Number>`, even though Integer extends Number. If it were, code could add a Double to your list of Integers through it. So a method that takes `List<Number>` rejects a `List<Integer>`.

A **wildcard** fixes that. `List<? extends Number>` accepts a list of Integer, Double, Long, or any other number type. You read each element as a `Number` and call `doubleValue()` on it, but the compiler stops you from adding to it, because it cannot know which number type the list really holds.

```java harness
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    {{code}}
    {
        double total = 0;
        for (Number value : values) {
            total += value.doubleValue();
        }
        return total;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        List<Integer> ints = List.of(4, 19, 7);
        List<Double> doubles = List.of(1.5, 2.25);
        System.out.printf("Int sum: %.2f%n", sumAll(ints));
        System.out.printf("Double sum: %.2f%n", sumAll(doubles));
    }
}
```

```java
static double sumAll(List<? extends Number> values)
```

Type arguments are **erased** after compiling, so at run time `T` is unknown. An array must know its element type when it is created, so `new T[n]` does not compile. Store items in a `List<T>` instead.

